NAME
    usb-image - distributable m-boot GPT built on GitHub x86_64

DESCRIPTION
    GitHub Actions workflow "Build complete bootable GPT image" reuses
    tests/qemu-combined-test.sh --prepare-only on a native Ubuntu 24.04
    x86_64 runner. It uses checked GRUB 2.12 tools and target module sets
    from the repository and publishes one complete 2 GiB disk image:
      1. 10 MiB BIOS Boot Partition EF02 (GRUB i386-pc embedded)
      2. 512 MiB FAT32 ESP (BOOTX64.EFI and BOOTAA64.EFI)
      3. shared exFAT loader (m/boot/loader)
    The workflow produces artifacts only, never writes a physical device.

DOWNLOAD AND VERIFY
    In GitHub Actions open "Build complete bootable GPT image", select the
    successful run and download its "m-boot-combined-gpt" artifact ZIP.
    Unzip into a new directory. The workflow retains artifacts for 30 days.
    Verify with:
        sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
        zstd -t m-boot-combined.raw.zst
        zstd -t m-boot-loader.tar.zst
        zstd -dc m-boot-combined.raw.zst | sha256sum
    Compare the last SHA256 with m-boot-combined.raw.sha256. The independent
    m-boot-loader.tar.zst stores the loader for restoring after exFAT
    reformatting. BUILD-INFO.txt identifies the exact source revision.

INITIAL USB FLASH (DESTRUCTIVE, REQUIRES PHYSICAL DEVICE AUTHORIZATION)
    A raw image flash destroys all existing data on the target USB device.
    The user must explicitly select the physical device and authorize
    writes. m-boot does not automatically flash arbitrary /dev/sdX paths.
    Verify a stable /dev/disk/by-id target and all of its partitions with
    lsblk and udev, ensure it is not mounted or used by the host, and
    confirm the device has 512-byte LOGICAL sectors and at least 2 GiB.
    Do not guess the disk identifier or flash the host's system disk.
    Physical flashing, repartitioning and formatting remain a distinct
    operator-authorized step, not a side effect of the build.

EXPAND THIRD PARTITION ON A NEWLY FLASHED USB
    The GPT backup header copied from a 2 GiB image must be moved to the
    end of the larger destination disk: sgdisk -e <verified-device>.
    Verify GPT again. GParted's published support matrix does NOT
    support growing existing exFAT filesystems. To use all remaining space,
    keep the BIOS and EFI partitions unchanged, record the first LBA of
    partition 3, delete/recreate ONLY partition 3 with the same first LBA,
    end at the actual end of the disk, and type 0700. After kernel
    partition re-probe, format ONLY the new third partition with exFAT
    (DESTRUCTIVE to partition 3), then restore the loader:
        zstd -dc m-boot-loader.tar.zst | tar -C <mounted-exfat-root> -xf -
    The archive contains m/boot/loader and no user data.

    Never perform this on an existing data-bearing disk. Do not move the
    beginning of EF02: embedded BIOS sector references depend on that
    location. Cloning copies disk and partition GUIDs, requiring deliberate
    uniqueness management for multiple simultaneous copies. A filesystem
    restore must be verified before relying on the USB to boot.

HOST PROCEDURE: VERIFIED IMAGE AND LINUX FLASH
    These are explicit operator steps, not an unattended installer. Work
    in the unzipped artifact directory. On Linux, require sha256sum and
    zstd. Decompress and check the raw 2 GiB image BEFORE choosing a disk:

        sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
        zstd -dc m-boot-combined.raw.zst > m-boot-combined.raw
        sha256sum -c m-boot-combined.raw.sha256
        stat -c %s m-boot-combined.raw

    The raw size must equal 2147483648 bytes. Confirm physical ownership
    of ALL existing content on the destination: flashing erases it.
    Replace the value below with the stable, manually identified symlink
    to the WHOLE intended disk, not a partition. There is deliberately
    no default destination or automatic disk-selection command.

        DISK=/dev/disk/by-id/REPLACE-WITH-VERIFIED-WHOLE-DISK
        test -b "$DISK"
        lsblk -o NAME,PATH,MODEL,SIZE,TYPE,TRAN,LOG-SEC,MOUNTPOINTS "$DISK"
        sudo blockdev --getss "$DISK"
        sudo blockdev --getsize64 "$DISK"

    Confirm the logical sector size is 512, the device has at least
    2147483648 bytes, NONE of its partitions is mounted/in use, and
    the device is NOT the running OS disk. Inspect again before executing
    this explicitly destructive command:

        sudo dd if=m-boot-combined.raw of="$DISK" bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress
        sync

    Do not run dd against a partition. Allow the OS to re-read the
    partition table before manipulating the freshly flashed device.

HOST PROCEDURE: macOS FLASH
    On macOS (including Apple Silicon), use diskutil list and diskutil
    info to positively identify a 512-byte-logical-sector physical disk
    and check all mounted volumes. After independently checking artifact
    checksums and decompressing the raw file using suitable installed
    tools, replace diskN/rdiskN with the SAME explicitly selected whole
    disk. The following is a template, not an automatically selected disk:

        diskutil list
        diskutil info /dev/diskN
        sudo diskutil unmountDisk /dev/diskN
        sudo dd if=m-boot-combined.raw of=/dev/rdiskN bs=4m
        sync
        diskutil eject /dev/diskN

    This only flashes the raw image. The partition recreation below
    requires a Linux environment with access to the actual physical disk
    (for example a Linux host or a carefully configured passthrough VM).
    A normal file-only VM disk does not modify the connected USB device.

HOST PROCEDURE: CONSUME REMAINING SPACE ON LINUX
    Run ONLY immediately after initial flashing, BEFORE writing any
    personal data. The exFAT filesystem copied from the 2 GiB image
    CANNOT simply be extended using standard GParted/exfatprogs.
    The operation below DESTROYS AND RECREATES partition 3's filesystem.
    Keep partitions 1 and 2 exactly where they are.

    With the same verified whole-disk DISK variable and all partitions
    unmounted, move the copied backup GPT to the actual disk end:

        sudo sgdisk -e "$DISK"
        sudo sgdisk --verify "$DISK"
        sudo sgdisk -i 1 "$DISK"
        sudo sgdisk -i 2 "$DISK"
        sudo sgdisk -i 3 "$DISK"

    Record partition 3's original FIRST LBA and confirm it agrees
    with the image layout (1071104 for this 2 GiB/512-byte image).
    This command reads the value; the explicit check refuses a different
    layout, rather than risk operating on an unrelated disk:

        start=$(sudo sgdisk -i 3 "$DISK" | sed -n 's/^First sector: \\([0-9][0-9]*\\).*$/\\1/p')
        test "$start" = 1071104

    Now delete/recreate ONLY the partition-3 GPT entry, retaining its
    first sector but extending its end to the last usable disk sector:

        sudo sgdisk -d 3 -n 3:"$start":0 -t 3:0700 "$DISK"
        sudo sgdisk --verify "$DISK"
        sudo partprobe "$DISK"
        sudo udevadm settle
        lsblk -o NAME,PATH,SIZE,TYPE,LOG-SEC,MOUNTPOINTS "$DISK"

    If partition re-reading fails, STOP: safely reattach/reboot and
    inspect the exact new table before proceeding. Independently select
    the whole disk's newly recreated PARTITION 3, never partition 1/2.
    It must have the recorded first sector and now reach the disk end.
    Replace this placeholder with the verified partition-3 block path:

        PART3=/dev/disk/by-id/REPLACE-WITH-VERIFIED-WHOLE-DISK-part3
        test -b "$PART3"
        sudo mkfs.exfat -n MBOOTDATA "$PART3"
        mountdir=$(mktemp -d)
        sudo mount "$PART3" "$mountdir"
        zstd -dc m-boot-loader.tar.zst | sudo tar -C "$mountdir" -xf -
        sudo test -s "$mountdir/m/boot/loader/init"
        sudo umount "$mountdir"
        rmdir "$mountdir"

    Check filesystem integrity and the final partition table before
    use. The original ESP and BIOS Boot Partition must be unchanged.
    Cloned disk/partition GUIDs should be made unique when simultaneous
    cloned media will be attached; deliberate GUID changes must be checked
    against any boot configuration that pins identifiers.

VALIDATION
    This workflow checks that real GRUB builds and GPT preparation succeed.
    It does not establish physical boot or automatic exFAT growth. Test
    the exact raw image under BIOS x86_64, UEFI x86_64 and UEFI ARM64
    with tests/qemu-run.sh. Hardware tests are separate.

